  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          Seville makes the accommodation question look easier than it is. The
          Cathedral, the Giralda, the Real Alcázar and the Archivo de Indias
          stand within four hundred metres of one another; the whole of the old
          city fits inside an oval about two kilometres across, flat, and walked
          end to end in half an hour. On that evidence any central room will do,
          and a great many booking pages are written on exactly that assumption.
        </p>
        <p>
          The assumption is wrong in a specific and expensive way. Because
          distance barely varies, everything else does the deciding — and the
          things that decide are the ones no map shows. Whether the lane outside
          your window is two metres wide and lined with bars. Whether the
          afternoon sun reaches your street at all. Whether a taxi can pull up
          at the door with your suitcase or stop ninety metres away on cobbles.
          Whether the walk home at one in the morning crosses a river. Seville's
          neighbourhoods are not further apart or closer to the sights in any
          way that matters. They are louder, hotter, dearer and quieter than one
          another, and those four differences are large.
        </p>
        <p>
          What follows assesses six areas on those terms. One of them carries a
          first-hand verdict: a nine-night stay in a street behind San Lorenzo,
          recorded on this site as
          <a href="/10-days-in-sevilla/">ten days using Seville as a base</a>,
          which also argues plainly against sleeping in Santa Cruz. The other
          five are assessed rather than remembered — from the city's geography,
          its published market and transport hours, and what the streets
          themselves are built like. Where a judgement rests on experience it is
          attributed. Where it rests on reasoning, it says so.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

